7. Open Problems

We catalogue the unresolved technical questions in order of priority.

· P0 – Fibrewise metric construction. The fibre bundle \mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{B} has been defined, but the precise form of the fibrewise metric g^{\mathcal{F}} (e.g., for uncertainty, consistency, and confidence coordinates) must be specified and justified. A natural candidate is to define these auxiliary coordinates as functionals of the belief and to equip the fibres with the push‑forward of a suitable Sobolev metric, but this remains to be carried out.
· P1 – Completeness and positivity of g_J. For Theorem 1 to guarantee global geodesics, (\mathcal{E}, g_J) must be complete. This requires h > V(x) uniformly on the domain of interest, a condition that may fail if the potential grows unboundedly. In the Gaussian example, the well is asymptotically linear, so completeness holds on the energy‑bounded set. General conditions need to be found.
· P2 – Adiabatic relaxation. Assumption A1 (stationary evidence) is strong. When the evidence stream varies slowly compared with the learning timescale, an adiabatic approximation may hold, in which the energy varies slowly and the trajectory approximately follows a sequence of Jacobi geodesics. This requires a careful averaging or modulation theory analysis.
· P3 – Geodesic convexity of V near T. Global convergence of the damped flow to the truth may fail if V is not geodesically convex in a neighbourhood of T. The Gaussian example has a convex V‑shaped potential along the submanifold, but in higher dimensions (e.g., when the mean is also updated) the hyperbolic geometry can produce multiple local minima. Characterising the basin of attraction is an open problem.
· P4 – Stochastic extensions. The addition of a Wiener noise term to (6) would yield an epistemic Langevin equation, whose Fokker–Planck equation might be related to continuous‑time Bayesian updating. Exploring fluctuation–dissipation relations could unify the geometric framework with probabilistic inference.
· P5 – Computational applications. The Jacobi metric g_J depends on the unknown truth T through \Delta. A learning agent would need to approximate this metric online. Deriving practical algorithms (e.g., a geodesic‑based optimiser with adaptive potential) is a natural next step.

---

8. Conclusion

The theory presented here elevates the “geodesic principle” of epistemic intelligence from an assertion to a theorem. By incorporating a kinetic term and applying the Jacobi–Maupertuis reduction, we have shown that, under a stationarity hypothesis, rational learning curves are geodesics of the deformed metric g_J = 2(h-V)g. The conformal factor explains how uncertainty and error warp the geometry of belief space to attract trajectories toward truth. The Gaussian location‑scale example validates the theory: it yields a precise threshold for overconfidence collapse, demonstrates that convergence requires dissipation, and recovers gradient descent in the overdamped limit. Several foundational questions remain open, most notably the full fibre‑bundle construction of the epistemic manifold and the relaxation of the stationarity assumption. Nevertheless, the present framework already constitutes a rigorous, falsifiable, and mathematically coherent foundation for the dynamics of knowledge.


