Appendix A. Riemannian Preliminaries

Let (\mathcal{E}, g) be a smooth Riemannian manifold of dimension n. The Levi‑Civita connection is denoted \nabla; for vector fields X,Y along a curve \gamma, the covariant derivative D X/dt \equiv \nabla_{\dot\gamma} X satisfies the metric compatibility and torsion‑freeness conditions. In local coordinates (x^i),

\frac{D X}{dt} = \left(\dot X^k + \Gamma^k_{ij} \dot\gamma^i X^j\right)\partial_k,

where \Gamma^k_{ij} = \frac12 g^{kl}(\partial_i g_{jl} + \partial_j g_{il} - \partial_l g_{ij}) are the Christoffel symbols.

A curve \gamma is a geodesic if D\dot\gamma/dt = 0. Geodesics are critical points of the length functional \int \|\dot\gamma\|_g\, dt and, with affine parametrisation, of the energy functional \int \|\dot\gamma\|_g^2 dt.

Lemma A.1 (Energy functional critical points).
A smooth curve \gamma is a geodesic iff it is a critical point of the energy functional E[\gamma] = \int_a^b \|\dot\gamma(t)\|_g^2 dt with respect to variations vanishing at endpoints, when the curve is parametrised proportionally to arclength.

Proof. Standard; see do Carmo, Riemannian Geometry, Chapter 1. ∎

---

Appendix B. Proof of Theorem 1 (Epistemic Geodesic Principle)

Theorem 1. Let (\mathcal{E},g) be a complete Riemannian manifold, V\in C^1(\mathcal{E}), and assume V has no explicit time‑dependence (Assumption A1). Fix an energy level h such that h > V(x) for all x in the region traversed. Define the Jacobi metric g_J = 2(h-V)g. Then a curve \gamma is a critical point of the action

S[\gamma] = \int \left(\frac12 \|\dot\gamma\|_g^2 - V(\gamma)\right) dt

with energy H = \frac12\|\dot\gamma\|_g^2 + V(\gamma) = h if and only if, after a suitable reparametrisation, its image is a geodesic of g_J.

Proof. We adapt Arnold’s derivation for \mathbb{R}^n to a Riemannian manifold. The Maupertuis action at fixed energy h is defined as

S_0[\gamma] = \int \|\dot\gamma\|_g \sqrt{2(h-V(\gamma))}\, dt,
\tag{B.1}

obtained by solving H=h for the kinetic term: \|\dot\gamma\|_g = \sqrt{2(h-V)}. The integral \int \|\dot\gamma\|_g \sqrt{2(h-V)} dt is precisely the length of \gamma with respect to the conformal metric g_J, since

\|\dot\gamma\|_{g_J} = \sqrt{g_J(\dot\gamma,\dot\gamma)} = \sqrt{2(h-V)}\, \|\dot\gamma\|_g.

Now, any solution of the Euler–Lagrange equations for the original action S with energy h satisfies the Maupertuis variational principle: the functional S_0 is stationary among curves with fixed energy h and fixed endpoints (see Arnold, §45). Conversely, a curve stationary for S_0 under the energy constraint can be reparametrised to satisfy H=h and the original equations. (The principle holds for any Lagrangian of the form L = \frac12 g_{ij}\dot x^i\dot x^j - V(x) on a manifold; the presence of the metric simply replaces the Euclidean inner product.)

To be fully explicit, consider variations \gamma_s with \gamma_0=\gamma and variation field W = \partial_s\gamma_s|_{s=0}. The first variation of S is

\delta S = \int \Bigl(g(\dot\gamma, \frac{DW}{dt}) - dV(W)\Bigr) dt.

Integrating by parts using metric compatibility and the fact that W vanishes at endpoints yields

\delta S = -\int \Bigl( g(\frac{D\dot\gamma}{dt}, W) + dV(W) \Bigr) dt,

so the Euler–Lagrange equation is \frac{D\dot\gamma}{dt} = -\nabla V (the gradient with respect to g).

The energy H = \frac12\|\dot\gamma\|_g^2 + V is conserved because

\frac{d}{dt}H = g(\frac{D\dot\gamma}{dt},\dot\gamma) + dV(\dot\gamma) = g(-\nabla V,\dot\gamma) + dV(\dot\gamma) = 0.

Now, consider the functional J[\gamma] = \int \sqrt{2(h-V(\gamma))} \|\dot\gamma\|_g dt. The integrand is positively homogeneous of degree 1 in \dot\gamma; hence its value is invariant under reparametrisation. Its Euler–Lagrange equation characterizes curves whose image is a geodesic of g_J. The constrained variation of S at fixed energy h is equivalent to the unconstrained variation of J (see Arnold, Chapter 3). Thus \gamma is a critical point of S with H=h iff its image is a geodesic for g_J. ∎

