Appendix C. Fibre Bundle Construction of the Epistemic Manifold

Let \mathcal{B} be a smooth manifold parametrising belief distributions. In many cases \mathcal{B} is a statistical manifold endowed with the Fisher–Rao metric g^{\mathcal{B}}. For each belief \theta\in\mathcal{B}, define a fibre \mathcal{F}_\theta = \{(U,C,L)\} where U is an uncertainty measure (e.g., entropy), C a consistency index, and L a confidence level. These are assumed to be smooth functions of the belief and possibly of the history; for the purpose of the manifold, we treat them as independent fibre coordinates so that \mathcal{E} is a fibre bundle with total space dimension \dim\mathcal{B} + \dim\mathcal{F}.

The metric on \mathcal{E} is defined as

g = \pi^* g^{\mathcal{B}} + g^{\mathcal{F}},

where g^{\mathcal{F}} is a fibrewise metric. A natural choice for g^{\mathcal{F}} is a constant Euclidean metric on the fibre coordinates, possibly scaled by a parameter that controls the relative cost of changing auxiliary variables. The Levi‑Civita connection of this direct sum metric preserves the horizontal and vertical subbundles; hence horizontal geodesics (with vertical velocity zero) project to geodesics of the base statistical manifold, while purely vertical motions are governed by g^{\mathcal{F}}.

For the Gaussian example in the main text, the fibre is trivial because we only consider the belief coordinates (\mu,\sigma) and the potential V is built directly from them. In general, the functionals \Phi and \Delta must be extended to \mathcal{E}: \Phi(x) can be taken as the entropy of the base belief, while \Delta(x) is the Riemannian distance from x to the truth state T, which may lie in a different fibre. This requires specifying the truth state’s fibre coordinates (e.g., the minimum possible uncertainty, perfect consistency). The theory remains flexible: one can fix U_T and C_T as the optimal values, and \Delta measures the distance in the total metric.

