Appendix F. Energy Dissipation and LaSalle Invariance Principle (Smooth Case)

For completeness, we recall the standard LaSalle invariance principle for smooth systems.

Theorem F.1 (LaSalle). Consider the autonomous differential equation \dot x = f(x) with f locally Lipschitz on a domain D \subset \mathbb{R}^n. Let H: D \to \mathbb{R} be a continuously differentiable function such that \dot H(x) \le 0 on D. Let E be the set of all points in D where \dot H(x) = 0, and let M be the largest invariant set contained in E. Then every bounded trajectory approaches M as t\to\infty.

In the damped geodesic case, the state is (w,\dot w), H = \frac12 \dot w^2 + V(w) (appropriately weighted). \dot H = -\rho \dot w^2 \le 0. E = \{(w,\dot w): \dot w = 0\}. The largest invariant set in E consists of points where \dot w = 0 and the equation of motion gives \ddot w = 0, thus V'(w)=0. If V has a unique critical point at w_T, then M = \{(w_T,0)\}. The boundedness of trajectories follows from energy decay and coercivity of V (which grows at infinity). For the Gaussian piecewise linear V, the energy sublevel sets are compact because V(w) \to \infty as |w|\to\infty and \dot w^2 adds. So convergence is global.

