Appendix G. Expected Uncertainty Contraction (Bayesian Updating)

Let p(\theta) be a prior distribution over a model parameter (belief) and p(x|\theta) the likelihood. The posterior after observing data x is p(\theta|x). Entropy of a distribution is H[p] = -\int p(\theta)\ln p(\theta) d\theta. The expected posterior entropy is

\mathbb{E}_{x}\left[ H[p(\cdot|x)] \right] = \int H[p(\cdot|x)] p(x) dx,

where p(x) = \int p(x|\theta) p(\theta) d\theta.

Theorem G.1 (Information never hurts, in expectation). For any prior p(\theta) and likelihood p(x|\theta), the expected posterior entropy satisfies \mathbb{E}_x[H[p(\cdot|x)]] \le H[p(\cdot)].

Proof. By the data processing inequality for KL divergence, or directly: The mutual information I(\Theta; X) = H[p(\theta)] - \mathbb{E}_x[H[p(\theta|x)]] \ge 0. Hence the difference is non‑negative. ∎

This is a theorem, not an axiom, providing a probabilistic foundation for Axiom 3.

